=== O10: the G_lambda weight audit -- every static-sector relation, weighted ===
  weights under G_lambda:  lam^1 = {a_0, g, g_N, g_c, rho_b, Sigma_c, M_b, kappa, div[mu grad Phi]},
                           lam^2 = {v_inf^4, |Pi|},   lam^0 = {G, c, rho_L, r, x, X, mu, K}

  A relation fixes kappa only if it MIXES weights.  Testing each for homogeneity:

      law (quadratic)   g_N = g^2/(g+a_0)          homogeneous of weight    1   -> FLAT DIRECTION
      mu                mu = g/(g+a_0)             homogeneous of weight    0   -> FLAT DIRECTION
      X_L               X(1+X) = x^2               homogeneous of weight    0   -> FLAT DIRECTION
      cold pull         g_c = a_0 g/(g+a_0)        homogeneous of weight    1   -> FLAT DIRECTION
      Poisson           div[mu grad Phi] = K rho_b homogeneous of weight    1   -> FLAT DIRECTION
      universal column  Sigma_c = a_0/(2 pi G)     homogeneous of weight    1   -> FLAT DIRECTION
      deep-field stress |Pi| = g^2/(8 pi G)        homogeneous of weight    2   -> FLAT DIRECTION
      BTFR              v_inf^4 = G M_b a_0        homogeneous of weight    2   -> FLAT DIRECTION
      definition        a_0 = kappa c sqrt(G rho_L) homogeneous of weight    1   -> FLAT DIRECTION

  9/9 relations are G_lambda-homogeneous, i.e. FLAT DIRECTIONS in kappa.
  The last one -- the definition -- is homogeneous of weight 1 too: that is exactly the
  statement 'kappa carries weight 1' (FINAL-16 J1 / FINAL-17 A4). It defines kappa; it
  does not value it.  No relation on the list mixes weights, so none can return a number.
  O10  the G_lambda no-go: no static-sector relation mixes weights, so none fixes kappa
       measured  : 9/9 relations G_lambda-homogeneous (flat directions); 0 mix weights
       threshold : all relations homogeneous -- the no-go confirmed, not assumed
       verdict   : PASS

